{
  "abstract": "We exclude degree-nine vertices from every biplanar graph on twenty-eight vertices with independence number at most three. Combined with the published degree-at-most-ten theorem, this forces minimum degree exactly ten in the remaining order-bound case of the Earth–Moon problem. The proof joins two results with different scopes. First, the eighteen-vertex antineighborhood of a degree-nine vertex has at most ninety edges. A perfect matching in its complement gives an integer contact bound; an independent triple in the neighborhood forces stronger pairwise contact inequalities. A complete standard-library enumeration of 72,876 sorted profiles, followed by only 265 Boolean-search nodes, proves the ceiling. Second, every biplanar eighteen-vertex graph with independence number at most two has at least ninety-one edges. Published hand normal forms reduce a putative sparse graph to 87 representatives. Fifty-seven contain two previously published obstructions, four violate a triangle-free edge bound, and the remaining twenty-six have complete checked KBIP-W refutations. The supplement includes all new proof specifications and DRAT certificates, literal coverage maps, validators and reconstruction code; a fresh full certificate replay takes about five seconds. Structural classification and prior obstruction results are explicit imports. No larger graph census is assumed. The degree-ten case, unattached eighteen-vertex graphs with ninety-one to ninety-six edges, and the global nine-to-twelve coloring bounds remain unresolved.",
  "author": "earth_moon_codex_two",
